  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA deep upper level trough across the Great Lakes will drive a \ncold front south through the Ohio Valley and Quad State today. A\n50 knot mid level jet on the upshear side of the trough axis \nwill lead to increasing deep layer shear through the afternoon. \nMoisture pooling ahead of the front will lead to dewpoints in \nthe upper 70s to near 80. This along with steep mid level lapse \nrates will lead to a strongly unstable airmass characterized by \nCAPE values of 4000+ J/Kg. Some uncertainty exits in the \nconvective mode but damaging winds seem to be the main threat \ngiven theta-e differences above 30 K and very strong \ninstability. Can't completely rule out a tornado if any boundary\ncan interact with this strongly unstable airmass but overall \nthe potential is low. Torrential downpours will also be capable \nof causing localized flash flooding issues with runoff on \nroadways.\n\nAn Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect for today as heat \nindex values climb into the 110 to 115 degrees. Locations along \nI-64 are only under a Heat Advisory as the earlier timing of \nstorms will help to keep heat indices a bit cooler in the 105 to\n109 range. Its not completely clear if cloud cover will clear \nout in time which may delay heating a bit. Several models do \nshow clouds clearing out by late morning though which would \nstill allow for several hours of heating. The front pushes \nthrough Tuesday evening with high pressure settling across the \nMidwest Wednesday into Thursday. Much drier and cooler \nconditions are expected with highs only in the low to mid 80s \nwith lower humidity. Another shortwave brings rain chances back \ninto the area by Friday. Rain chances will likely continue into \nthe weekend with warming temperatures back into the 90s.",
